::Curated_OS_035::[plain]Which of the following is NOT a requirement for a correct solution to the critical section problem? {~Bounded waiting must be guaranteed ~Progress must be guaranteed =Priority inversion must be prevented ~Mutual exclusion must be guaranteed}
####Source: Curated - Process Management

::Curated_OS_036::[plain]Peterson's solution to the critical section problem satisfies {=mutual exclusion, progress, and bounded waiting ~mutual exclusion and bounded waiting only ~only mutual exclusion ~only mutual exclusion and progress}
####Source: Curated - Process Management

::Curated_OS_037::[plain]Internal fragmentation occurs when {=allocated memory is larger than the requested memory ~the TLB is flushed on a context switch ~the page table grows beyond one page ~free memory blocks are too small to satisfy a request}
####Source: Curated - Process Management

::Curated_OS_038::[plain]Compaction is a technique used to address {~internal fragmentation =external fragmentation ~page faults ~thrashing}
####Source: Curated - Process Management

::Curated_OS_039::[plain]Thrashing occurs when {=the system spends more time servicing page faults than executing processes ~the CPU utilization is 100 percent ~the degree of multiprogramming is too low ~the page size is too large}
####Source: Curated - Process Management

::Curated_OS_040::[plain]The working set model is used to {~recover from deadlocks =estimate the set of pages a process needs in the near future to control thrashing ~allocate disk blocks to files ~schedule processes by priority}
####Source: Curated - Process Management

::Curated_OS_041::[plain]Which of the following are necessary conditions for deadlock? {~%33.33%Hold and wait ~%33.33%Mutual exclusion ~%33.33%Circular wait ~%-100%Preemption}
####Source: Curated - Process Management

::Curated_OS_042::[plain]A system has a 24-bit virtual address space and 4 KB pages. The number of page table entries in a single-level page table is ________. {#4096}
####Source: Curated - Memory Management

::Curated_OS_043::[plain]In round robin scheduling, if the time quantum is larger than the largest burst time, the algorithm behaves like {=FCFS ~SJF ~Priority scheduling ~SRTF}
####Source: Curated - Memory Management

::Curated_OS_044::[plain]When a process executes a P (wait) operation on a counting semaphore whose value is 0, the process {~continues execution normally ~receives an error =blocks until another process performs a V (signal) operation ~terminates immediately}
####Source: Curated - Memory Management

